      "id": "cfea1f4e-a1e3-4b49-8d91-abecf0b3cbc3",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "You’re at **the most important conceptual step** in lambdas: **method references**.\nLet’s walk through this code slowly and *visually in your head*, because once this clicks, **Streams and Spring code become readable**.\n\n---\n\n# 🧠 What problem do method references solve?\n\nLambdas can feel noisy when they only **call an existing method**.\n\nExample:\n\n```java\nx -> System.out.println(x)\n```\n\nJava lets you replace this with:\n\n```java\nSystem.out::println\n```\n\nThat’s **all** a method reference is.\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ The `PlainOld` class (why it exists)\n\n```java\nclass PlainOld {\n\n    private static int last_id = 1;\n    private int id;\n\n    public PlainOld() {\n        id = PlainOld.last_id++;\n        System.out.println(\"Creating a PlainOld Object: id = \" + id);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Purpose\n\n* Has a **no-argument constructor**\n* Prints when an object is created\n* Lets you *see* when Java calls `new PlainOld()`\n\nThis is perfect for learning **constructor references**.\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Method reference to an instance method\n\n```java\nlist.forEach(System.out::println);\n```\n\n### Expand it mentally\n\n```java\nlist.forEach(s -> System.out.println(s));\n```\n\n### Why this works\n\n* `forEach` expects a `Consumer<String>`\n* `println(String)` matches:\n\n```java\nvoid accept(String s)\n```\n\n### Syntax pattern\n\n```\nobject::instanceMethod\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```java\nSystem.out::println\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ Static method reference\n\n```java\ncalculator(Integer::sum, 10, 25);\ncalculator(Double::sum, 2.5, 7.5);\n```\n\n### Expand it mentally\n\n```java\ncalculator((a, b) -> Integer.sum(a, b), 10, 25);\ncalculator((a, b) -> Double.sum(a, b), 2.5, 7.5);\n```\n\n### Why it matches\n\n`calculator` expects:\n\n```java\nBinaryOperator<T>\nT apply(T a, T b);\n```\n\nAnd `Integer.sum(int, int)`:\n\n* takes two ints\n* returns an int\n\nPerfect match.\n\n### Syntax pattern\n\n```\nClassName::staticMethod\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ Constructor reference (this is the cool part)\n\n```java\nSupplier<PlainOld> reference1 = PlainOld::new;\n```\n\n### Expand it mentally\n\n```java\nSupplier<PlainOld> reference1 = () -> new PlainOld();\n```\n\n### Mapping\n\n| Supplier method | Constructor      |\n| --------------- | ---------------- |\n| `get()`         | `new PlainOld()` |\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ Object is created ONLY when `get()` is called\n\n```java\nPlainOld newPojo = reference1.get();\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nCreating a PlainOld Object: id = 1\n```\n\nThis proves:\n\n* The constructor is **not called earlier**\n* It’s **lazy creation**\n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ Constructor reference passed as behavior\n\n```java\nPlainOld[] pojo1 = seedArray(PlainOld::new, 10);\n```\n\n### Method definition\n\n```java\nprivate static PlainOld[] seedArray(Supplier<PlainOld> reference, int count) {\n    PlainOld[] array = new PlainOld[count];\n    Arrays.setAll(array, i -> reference.get());\n    return array;\n}\n```\n\n### What happens step by step\n\n1. `Arrays.setAll` loops over array indices\n2. For each index:\n\n   ```java\n   reference.get()\n   ```\n3. Which becomes:\n\n   ```java\n   new PlainOld()\n   ```\n\n### Output\n\n```\nCreating a PlainOld Object: id = 2\nCreating a PlainOld Object: id = 3\n...\nCreating a PlainOld Object: id = 11\n```\n\nExactly **10 objects** created.\n\n---\n\n## 7️⃣ Why not just use `new PlainOld()` inside the method?\n\nBecause this design means:\n\n> “You tell me *how* to create an object, I’ll decide *when* to create it.”\n\nThis is the foundation of:\n\n* Dependency Injection\n* Factories\n* Spring bean creation\n\n---\n\n## 8️⃣ The 4 types of method references (MEMORIZE THIS)\n\n### 1️⃣ Static method\n\n```java\nClassName::staticMethod\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```java\nInteger::sum\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2️⃣ Instance method of a specific object\n\n```java\nobject::instanceMethod\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```java\nSystem.out::println\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3️⃣ Instance method of an arbitrary object\n\n```java\nClassName::instanceMethod\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```java\nString::toUpperCase\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4️⃣ Constructor\n\n```java\nClassName::new\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```java\nPlainOld::new\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Final mental model (lock this in 🔒)\n\n> **If your lambda only calls one method,\n> you can almost always replace it with a method reference.**\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 Why this matters\n\nYou now understand:\n\n* Why Java code looks “weird but short”\n* How Streams work\n* How Spring injects objects\n* How clean functional Java is written\n\n---\n\nIf you want next:\n\n* convert lambdas → method references step-by-step\n* explain confusing cases like `String::compareTo`\n* show **Stream API examples using method references**\n\nJust tell me 👍\n",
